<section class="section-tight" style="padding-top:0">
  <div class="wrap bi-grid">
    <article class="article">
      <div class="article-img"><img src="../assets/img/blog/landing-page-rehberi.svg" alt=""></div>
      <p class="bi-lead">If you send people who click on your Google Ads or social media ads to your homepage, you are most likely wasting part of your budget. A <b>landing page</b> is a page that keeps the ad’s promise on a single page and guides visitors to a single action.</p>
      <p>A homepage tries to cover everything: services, about us, blog, contact. A visitor coming from an ad, however, is looking for something specific and gets lost in that crowd. A good landing page shows that visitor exactly what they are looking for and makes the next step easy.</p>
      <p>In this guide we answer what a landing page is, what types there are and which components it can’t work without; then we explain how to build a landing page step by step, its relationship with ads and SEO, and common mistakes.</p>
      <h2 id="nedir" class="bi-h2" data-n="01">What is a landing page, and how is it different from a homepage?</h2>
      <p>A landing page is the page a visitor reaches after clicking on an ad, email, social media post or campaign link, focusing on <b>a single offer and a single goal</b>. Technically, any page on your site can be the “first page opened”; but in marketing, a landing page means a page designed specifically for this purpose.</p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Homepage</th><th>Landing page</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Introducing the company in general</td><td>Getting one action done</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Target audience</td><td>Everyone</td><td>The audience of a specific campaign</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Links</td><td>Menu, many internal links</td><td>Few or no outbound links; a single primary button</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Content</td><td>A summary of all services</td><td>A single product, service or offer</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Measurement</td><td>General traffic and engagement</td><td>A clear conversion rate</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p>In short, the homepage is a store’s shop window, while a landing page is a stand set up for a specific product, staffed with a sales consultant.</p>
      <h2 id="turler" class="bi-h2" data-n="02">Types of landing pages by goal</h2>
      <p>The structure of the page depends on what you want from the visitor. The most common types are:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Lead generation page:</b> Collects contact details in exchange for a quote form, free assessment, demo request or e-book. It is the most common type in the service sector.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Sales page:</b> Sells a single product or package directly. It is usually longer and answers objections one by one.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Click-through page:</b> Introduces the product and sends the visitor to the checkout or sign-up page already “warmed up”. Common in e-commerce campaigns.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Event and webinar registration page:</b> Date, speakers, agenda and a registration form.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>App download page:</b> Shows the benefits of a mobile app and directs visitors to the App Store and Google Play buttons.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>Choosing a type makes every decision on the page easier: a form or a button? A short page or a long one?</p>
      <h2 id="bilesenler" class="bi-h2" data-n="03">The essential components of an effective landing page</h2>
      <p>Whatever the industry, well-performing landing pages almost always include these components:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A headline that matches the ad:</b> A benefit-focused main headline that repeats the promise in the ad the visitor clicked.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>An explanatory subheading:</b> Makes the offer concrete in one sentence: for whom, what, how.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A strong visual:</b> The product itself, a screenshot, a results photo or a short promo video.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A list of benefits:</b> Three to five points describing the results the visitor will get, not the features.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Social proof:</b> Customer reviews, client logos, number of completed projects, ratings.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A single, clear call to action:</b> A button describing the outcome, such as “Get a free quote” or “Schedule a demo”; on long pages the same button can be repeated several times.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A short form:</b> Only the information you really need.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Frequently asked questions:</b> A short section answering objections at the moment of decision, such as price, timeline and warranty.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>We cover the general UX principles behind these components in more detail in our article on <a href="donusum-odakli-web-tasarim">conversion-focused web design</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="adimlar" class="bi-h2" data-n="04">How do you build a landing page? A 7-step process</h2>
      <p>The process we follow when building landing pages in our projects consists of these seven steps:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 1 — Set a single goal.</b> If this page succeeds, what will the visitor have done? Fill in a form, call, or buy? Choose one answer.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 2 — Define the audience and traffic source.</b> Someone coming from a Google search looking for a solution to their problem and someone who saw your ad on Instagram have different levels of knowledge; the page should speak accordingly.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 3 — Clarify the offer.</b> Why act now? A free assessment, a limited-time discount, a trial period or a concrete guarantee.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 4 — Write the copy first.</b> Don’t move on to design until the headline, benefits, objection answers and button copy are ready. Design is a frame that carries the copy.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 5 — Design mobile-first.</b> Most ad traffic comes from phones. Plan the phone screen first, then expand to desktop.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 6 — Set up measurement.</b> Define form submissions and phone clicks as conversions in analytics and the ads dashboard; add campaign parameters (UTM) to your links.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Step 7 — Publish and test.</b> The first version is never the final one. As data comes in, improve the headline, visual and form through testing.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M13 2L4 14h7l-1 8 9-12h-7l1-8z"/></svg><div><b>Quick tip:</b> Build a separate landing page for each campaign or service. Sending someone who came from a “boiler maintenance” ad to a generic “our services” page forces them to search again to find what they wanted.</div></div>
      <h2 id="reklam-uyumu" class="bi-h2" data-n="05">Landing pages and Google Ads: the page experience that affects Quality Score</h2>
      <p>When evaluating your ads in search campaigns, Google Ads uses a diagnostic tool called Quality Score. According to Google’s help page, Quality Score has three components: expected click-through rate, ad relevance and <b>landing page experience</b>. Landing page experience reflects how relevant and useful your page is to users who click your ad, and it is rated “above average”, “average” or “below average” compared with other advertisers showing ads for the same search.</p>
      <p>In practice, this means your landing page directly affects how efficiently your ad budget works. To improve page experience:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Message match:</b> If your ad says “same-day installation”, the first screen of the page should say so too.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Keyword-specific content:</b> Use different pages, or at least different headlines, for different search intents.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Transparency:</b> Provide easy access to company details, contact channels and your privacy policy.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Speed and mobile-friendliness:</b> A page that loads fast and is easy to navigate on mobile protects both the user and ad performance.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>As for the question of ads versus organic traffic, we compare the two in our article <a href="google-ads-mi-seo-mu">Google Ads or SEO</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="seo" class="bi-h2" data-n="06">Landing pages and SEO: using landing pages for organic traffic</h2>
      <p>Landing pages aren’t just for ads; a well-structured service page can also be a strong landing page in organic search. But there are a few rules to know:</p>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Avoid thin content:</b> Pages consisting only of a headline and a form may work for ads but won’t rank in organic search. If you have an organic goal, the page should have rich content that genuinely explains the topic.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>The doorway page trap:</b> Google’s spam policies explicitly define intermediate pages created to rank for similar searches and funnel users to the actual page as a violation. Pages duplicated city by city with almost identical text may fall into this category.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Keep campaign copies out of the index:</b> Keeping near-identical pages created for A/B tests or short-term campaigns out of search results with “noindex” prevents duplicate content confusion.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <p>We cover the basics of SEO from the ground up in our article <a href="seo-nedir">What is SEO</a>.</p>
      <h2 id="test" class="bi-h2" data-n="07">A/B testing and measurement: how do you improve a landing page?</h2>
      <p>The best thing about a landing page is that it is measurable: since there is a single goal, you track success with a single rate. The way to improve that rate is regular testing.</p>
      <h3>What should you test?</h3>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Headline:</b> Benefit-focused or problem-focused? This is usually where the biggest impact comes from.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Call to action:</b> Button copy, colour and position.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Form length:</b> Fewer fields may bring more leads; more fields, better-qualified leads. Which is more valuable to you?</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Visual:</b> A product screen, a photo of people, or a video?</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Page length:</b> Long pages usually work better for expensive, considered services, and short pages for quick decisions; but test this too.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Rules for proper testing</h3>
      <p>Change only one variable at a time, run both versions during the same period with similar traffic, and don’t decide until enough data has accumulated. By adding UTM parameters to campaign links, you can see separately in Google Analytics which ad and channel brought conversions.</p>
      <blockquote><div class="bi-q"><svg class="ic" width="22" height="22" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M9 7H5a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3M19 7h-4a1 1 0 0 0-1 1v4a1 1 0 0 0 1 1h3v1a3 3 0 0 1-3 3"/></svg></div>A good landing page isn’t designed; it’s found through testing.</blockquote>
      <h2 id="hatalar" class="bi-h2" data-n="08">6 common mistakes when building a landing page</h2>
      <ul class="bi-list">
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Sending ad traffic to the homepage:</b> Visitors have to wander around the menu to find what they want.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Multiple goals:</b> A quote form, a newsletter sign-up and a catalogue download on the same page split attention.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>A message that doesn’t match the ad:</b> Promising a discount in the ad and not mentioning it on the page instantly breaks trust.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Slow loading:</b> Heavy videos and unoptimised images lose visitors before the page even opens.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Publishing without measurement:</b> Launching a campaign without conversion tracking means spending money without knowing which penny worked.</span></li>
        <li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span><b>Forgetting legal texts:</b> If you collect personal data through a form, a KVKK privacy notice and, where necessary, explicit consent must be added. This note is not legal advice; consult a legal professional for details.</span></li>
      </ul>
      <div class="bi-note"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l8 3v6c0 4.5-3.4 8.3-8 9-4.6-.7-8-4.5-8-9V6l8-3z"/><path d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"/></svg><div><b>Warning:</b> Off-the-shelf landing page tools offer a quick start, but the page usually sits on another domain or on infrastructure outside your control. For long-running campaigns, publishing the page on your own site and domain is healthier for both trust and measurement.</div></div>
      <div class="bi-check">
        <div class="eyebrow">// PRE-LAUNCH CHECKLIST</div>
        <ul class="bi-list"><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the page have a single goal and a single primary button?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the headline match the promise in the ad exactly?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Have the mobile view and load speed been tested?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Does the form contain only the necessary fields?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are conversion tracking and UTM parameters set up?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Is there a KVKK privacy notice link on the form?</span></li><li><svg class="ic" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 12l5 5 9-10"/></svg><span>Are a thank-you page and the next step defined?</span></li></ul>
      </div>
      <h2 id="sss" class="bi-h2" data-n="09">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="bi-faq"><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What is the difference between a landing page and a website?</summary><p>A website is a multi-page structure that introduces your company and all your services. A landing page is a page prepared for a specific campaign or offer, focused on a single goal and guiding the visitor to a single action. A landing page usually sits within your website.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>How long should a landing page be?</summary><p>It depends on the complexity and price of the offer. A short page is enough for a free e-book or a simple sign-up, while for expensive, considered services a longer page that answers objections usually performs better. Testing gives the most accurate answer.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Do landing pages contribute to SEO?</summary><p>A landing page that covers the topic thoroughly and is genuinely useful to users can also rank in organic search. However, ad pages consisting only of a form and a short headline, and pages duplicated with nearly identical content, usually don’t succeed in organic search.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>What tools are used to build landing pages?</summary><p>You can use off-the-shelf drag-and-drop landing page tools, content management systems like WordPress, or pages custom-coded for your site. Off-the-shelf tools are practical for quick tests; for long-running campaigns, pages published on your own site have the advantage in speed, measurement and brand consistency.</p></details><details class="bi-faq-i"><summary>Do I need a separate landing page for each ad?</summary><p>Not for each ad, but a separate landing page is recommended for each different offer, service or search intent. That way the message in the ad and the message on the page match exactly, which positively affects both the conversion rate and the landing page experience in Google Ads.</p></details></div>
      <h2 id="sonuc">Final word</h2>
      <p>A landing page consists of a single goal, a message that matches the ad, a persuasive offer and an easy action. Built right, it increases the results you get from your ad budget; built wrong, it wastes even the best campaign. Most importantly, publish the first version and keep improving it based on data.</p>
      <p>If you would like fast-loading landing pages with measurement in place and in line with your brand for your campaigns, <a href="iletisim">get in touch with us</a>. You can explore our design and development services on our <a href="web-cozumleri">web solutions</a> page.</p>
